## Test it

```bash
go test -race ./...
```

`internal/modules/user/module_test.go` boots the whole graph and drives the
handler through `App.Invoke`, then asserts the event reached the broker
through the outbox relay. No Docker, no network, no sleeps.

## What it serves

```
POST /users     the RegisterUser use case             201
GET  /healthz   liveness — runs no checks             200
GET  /readyz    readiness — lifecycle + every check   200 / 503
```

Every response carries `X-Correlation-Id`; a validation failure is a 400 with
per-field `details`. None of that is code in this repository — it comes from
the controller registering one route.

## What is not here yet

**No gRPC adapter**, and no Postgres wiring: the repository is in-memory. To
add Postgres, put `postgres.Module(postgres.DSN(cfg.DatabaseURL))` in
`cmd/{{.Name}}/main.go` and follow the three repository rules in warren.md
§6.1 — `RequireTx` first, `db(ctx)` for the handle, `persistence.Track` to
enlist. Everything else works: use cases, the module graph, config, the
transactional outbox, the consumer chain, health.

## Layout

```
cmd/{{.Name}}/            main: the module graph and Run()
internal/config/          the app's Config struct
internal/platform/        broker, outbox, unit of work, relay, health
internal/modules/user/    a feature: domain, application, infrastructure
internal/modules/notification/
                          a second feature, consuming the first's events
```

`internal/modules/<name>/module.go` is the only file permitted to see all
four layers. `domain` imports nothing from the others — that rule is why the
domain can be tested, versioned, and extracted on its own.
